# jemdoc: menu{menu.jemdoc}{joining.html}, title{Joining the Group}, notime

I supervise pre-thesis and thesis projects for students in the School of
Computer Science and Engineering. This page explains what I work on, what
kinds of topics are available, and how supervision runs.

== Co-supervision

Some projects are run jointly with a colleague, in which case there are two
supervisors and the scope is agreed between the three of us. Mention it in
your message if you would like to be considered for one of those.

- *Dr. Toan-Van Nguyen* — San Diego State University, USA · vannguyentoan@gmail.com
- *Assoc. Prof. Tung Kieu* — Aalborg University, Denmark · tungkvt@cs.aau.dk
- *M.Sc. Nguyen Trung Nghia* — International University, Vietnam · ntnghia@hcmiu.edu.vn

== What I work on

My current interests include deep learning, federated learning, edge inference,
collaborative inference, semantic communication, large language models (LLMs),
distributed learning, deep reinforcement learning for networks, and wireless
and mobile networking with the Internet of Things.

The easiest way to find a topic is to open the two or three most recent papers
on the [publications.html Publications] page and ask which one you would like
to understand deeply enough to extend.

== Open topics

- *Deep Learning and Large Language Models:* applications in network
  optimization and semantic communication.
- *Federated Learning and Collaborative Inference:* distributed training and
  inference across resource-constrained edge devices.
- *Edge Intelligence and Semantic Communication:* transmitting semantic meaning
  rather than raw bits over limited bandwidth.
- *Deep Reinforcement Learning for Networks:* learning control policies for
  wireless and mobile networking without accurate models.
- *Network Optimization and Game Theory:* resource allocation and network
  utility maximization.

== How we work

- One individual meeting each week, plus a group seminar.
- You are expected to read actively, write down what you understand, and
  present your work regularly — including the weeks when it is not going well.
- The goal is a result you can defend: a clear question, a method, and evidence.